From 676d40529c0c591dff14f23574e8fe75366ed71c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 08:14:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] assurance.fleet: advisory fan-out to serial numbers, and the
 declaration binding
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

WHY
  A supplier publishes an advisory about one firmware version. It lands in an
  inbox. The integrator who carries the CE liability for forty cells cannot
  answer which of them is running it — the mailing list does not know what is
  installed anywhere, the plant does not know what its cells are made of, and
  the supplier does not know who bought what. The manifest fleet is the only
  place that map exists, and because the manifest already maps item to safety
  function to verification check, the answer reaches past serial numbers to the
  evidence the advisory unsettles. This is the layer that gets more valuable
  with every machine enrolled.

WHAT
  src/assurance/fleet/registry.py     every machine in the ledger, worst first
  src/assurance/fleet/advisory.py     component advisories, and a matcher that
                                       reports what the match rests on
  src/assurance/fleet/impact.py       the fan-out: advisory x fleet -> serial
                                       numbers -> safety functions -> coverage
  src/assurance/fleet/declaration.py  the Declaration of Conformity, bound to a
                                       configuration hash so it can be shown to
                                       have stopped describing the machine
  src/assurance/fleet/cli.py          list / advisory / declare / declaration
  src/assurance/api/fleet_routes.py   five endpoints, two of them free
  examples/fleet/                     a real advisory and a declaration
  deploy/assurance/FLEET.md           how it matches and what it refuses to say

CUSTOMER VALUE
  "CTRL-2026-11: 4 of your 5 machines match. AR-7#0412 at Plant 2 Line 4 is
  confirmed by hash, and SF-01 and SF-02 — verified 9 February, standing until
  this morning — are now in question." Nobody can produce that today, and every
  integrator shipping collaborative cells needs it before January 2027.

  And the finding nothing else on the market can make: AR-7#0418 reports the
  affected version and its artefact does not hash to what the supplier
  published for it. Neither affected nor clear. Somebody has to go and look.

RISK
  Matching is where this goes wrong, so the basis is never flattened to a
  boolean: hash / hash_mismatch / version / name_only, each with its confidence
  and whether a human is needed. No version-range arithmetic — vendor schemes
  are not semver and parsing them as if they were is how a cell gets cleared
  that should have been stopped. A function already stale is not counted as
  newly in question, because that would hide the machines that were fine until
  this morning. And the declaration check makes no legal judgement: whether a
  change is a substantial modification is for the manufacturer and their
  notified body, and it says so in every output.

ROLLBACK
  git revert this commit. Outside assurance/fleet it touches plans.py copy,
  one router in service.py, one CLI domain and one ruff ignore.

TESTS
  453 passed (53 new), ruff clean over src/assurance and the new tests.
  The mixed-fleet case — confirmed, contradictory, part-stale, clear and
  never-verified in one ledger — is asserted column by column.

SUCCESS CONDITION
  One integrator forwards a supplier bulletin into this and gets back serial
  numbers they were going to find out about from a customer instead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
